      </div>

      <p>
        A provincial nomination adds the province's own fee; in B.C. the Skills Immigration application fee is $1,750. Our <a href="/articles/canada-pr-application-fees-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">PR application fees guide</a> explains how and when these fees change.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Costs IRCC does not set</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Medical exams for every family member,</strong> including those not coming, done by an IRCC panel physician. Prices vary by country and clinic.</li>
        <li><strong>Police certificates</strong> for each adult, from each country where they lived for the period IRCC requires.</li>
        <li><strong>Language tests</strong> for the principal applicant and, if it will earn points, the spouse. Results are valid for two years.</li>
        <li><strong>Educational credential assessments</strong> for foreign education you claim points for, including the spouse's.</li>
        <li><strong>Translations, flights, temporary housing and private health insurance</strong> for any provincial waiting period.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="plan" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How Can We Move to Canada? A 12–24 Month Household Plan</h2>

      <p>
        These stages assume a permanent-first plan through Express Entry or a PNP. Timings are planning guides, not guarantees. For stage-by-stage processing times, see <a href="/articles/how-long-to-get-pr-in-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how long it takes to get PR</a>.
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-4">
        <li><strong>Months 1–2: Map the household.</strong> List every family member, including children from earlier relationships and any spouse who will not come. Note each child's birth date against the under-22 rule. Order passports for anyone who needs one.</li>
        <li><strong>Months 1–3: Test both partners.</strong> Book IELTS General Training, CELPIP-General or PTE Core for English, or TEF Canada or TCF Canada for French. Test the spouse too if their score would earn points.</li>
        <li><strong>Months 2–4: Assess credentials.</strong> Get educational credential assessments for each partner's foreign degrees that will earn points.</li>
        <li><strong>Month 4: Decide the principal applicant.</strong> Calculate CRS both ways (and, after November 3, 2026, under the new scoring). If a provincial job offer is involved, the job holder is usually the principal. Use our <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a> if B.C. is an option.</li>
        <li><strong>Months 4–6: Enter the pool.</strong> Create the Express Entry profile and any provincial registration. Start saving or documenting proof of funds if you need it.</li>
        <li><strong>Months 5–12: Wait for an invitation and pre-collect documents.</strong> Gather birth and marriage certificates, proof of common-law relationship, custody papers or consent from a child's other parent, and employment letters. Request police certificates that take a long time to arrive.</li>
        <li><strong>After the invitation: Submit within the deadline.</strong> Express Entry gives 60 days. Book medicals for everyone, including non-accompanying members, and give biometrics together.</li>
        <li><strong>Months 12–20: Processing.</strong> IRCC aims to finish 80% of complete Express Entry applications in six months; non-Express Entry PNP files usually take longer. Report any change to IRCC right away: a marriage, a birth, a divorce, a new address.</li>
        <li><strong>Months 18–24: Land and settle.</strong> Travel before your confirmation of PR expires. Apply for provincial health cards and Social Insurance Numbers, and register children in school.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-documents-checklist" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">documents checklist</a> lists the family documents provinces typically ask for.
      </p>

      <h2 id="kids" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Children: School and Health Coverage</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">School</h3>
      <p>
        Public elementary and secondary school is free for children who are permanent residents or citizens, so landing as PR settles that question everywhere. Under federal law, a minor child in Canada can attend preschool, primary or secondary school without a study permit unless the parent is a temporary resident who is not authorized to work or study. Whether a temporary resident's child pays tuition depends on the province, the school district and the parent's permit, so confirm with the district before you move. Post-secondary study is different: a child who is not yet a PR needs a study permit and usually pays international fees.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Health coverage</h3>
      <p>
        Each province runs its own health plan and sets its own waiting period. In British Columbia, coverage for newcomers starts after the rest of the month in which you establish residence plus two more months, so arriving on April 5 means coverage from July 1. Ontario says there is no longer a waiting period for OHIP. Other provinces differ. Buy private insurance for every family member to cover any gap, and apply for health cards as soon as you arrive.
      </p>

      <h2 id="mistakes" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Mistakes That Hurt Families</h2>

      <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-2">The most serious one: an undeclared family member</p>
        <p class="text-red-800">Paragraph 117(9)(d) of the Immigration and Refugee Protection Regulations says a person is not a member of the family class if the sponsor previously applied for and became a permanent resident and, at that time, the person was a non-accompanying family member who was not examined. In plain terms: if you immigrate without declaring and examining your spouse or child, you generally can never sponsor them later. The only exception is when an officer decided an exam was not required, and even that does not apply if you could have made them available and did not, or if they were a separated spouse. Undeclared family members can also be treated as misrepresentation, with refusal and a five-year ban.</p>
      </div>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Leaving out a "difficult" family member.</strong> A child who lives with an ex-partner, a spouse you are separated from but not divorced from, a child born after you applied. All must be declared and, normally, examined.</li>
        <li><strong>Marrying or having a baby after your PR is approved but before landing, without telling IRCC.</strong> Tell IRCC before you travel. A new spouse or child who was never examined can fall under the same exclusion.</li>
        <li><strong>Picking the principal applicant by habit.</strong> The partner with the higher-paid job is not always the higher-scoring candidate.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing the age lock-in.</strong> Delaying a provincial application by a few weeks can cost a 21-year-old their place on the family's application.</li>
        <li><strong>Counting only the people who are moving.</strong> Proof of funds and medical exams cover everyone in the family unit.</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming the spouse can work.</strong> Under 2025 rules, many spouses of students and workers no longer qualify for open work permits. Check the actual eligibility before you rely on two incomes.</li>
        <li><strong>Letting someone's status lapse.</strong> For in-Canada families, each person's permit has its own expiry date. Track all of them.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="faq" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">How can we immigrate to Canada as a family?</h3>
          <p>Most families apply on one permanent residence application. One person is the principal applicant and qualifies through a program such as Express Entry, a Provincial Nominee Program or the Atlantic Immigration Program. The spouse or common-law partner and dependent children are included on the same application and become permanent residents at the same time. If a close relative is already a Canadian citizen or permanent resident, family sponsorship may also be possible.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Can my spouse and I both apply, and who should be the principal applicant?</h3>
          <p>Only one of you is the principal applicant on the permanent residence application, and the other is included as the accompanying spouse. Choose the person who scores higher or who qualifies for a stronger program: usually the partner who is younger, has higher language scores, more education or Canadian work experience, or a qualifying job offer. In Express Entry, each partner can usually create a profile listing the other as an accompanying spouse, but only one application goes ahead, and both profiles must be accurate and consistent.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Can I include my children on my Canadian PR application?</h3>
          <p>Yes. A child is a dependent child if they are under 22 and do not have a spouse or common-law partner. Children 22 or older can qualify only if they have depended on their parents since before age 22 and cannot support themselves because of a physical or mental condition. Age is locked in on a set date, which for a Provincial Nominee Program is the date the province receives your complete nomination application, and for Express Entry programs is the date IRCC receives your complete PR application.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">How much money do we need to immigrate to Canada with family?</h3>
          <p>For Express Entry applicants who must show settlement funds, the minimum is $23,360 for a family of three and $28,362 for a family of four, based on amounts IRCC updated on July 7, 2025, which were still listed as of October 2026. On top of that, IRCC fees for two adults and two children total $3,720 including the right of permanent residence fee, plus $170 for family biometrics. Medical exams, police certificates, language tests and any provincial fees are extra.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Do family members who are not coming to Canada need a medical exam?</h3>
          <p>Yes. IRCC says family members must have a medical exam even if they are not coming with you, and they must be declared on your application. Skipping the exam for a family member can make your application refused, and a family member who was not examined generally cannot be sponsored by you later.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">What happens if I do not declare a family member?</h3>
          <p>Under paragraph 117(9)(d) of the Immigration and Refugee Protection Regulations, a family member who was not declared and examined when you immigrated is excluded from the family class. In practice, you generally can never sponsor that spouse or child later. Failing to declare a family member can also be treated as misrepresentation, which can lead to refusal and a five-year ban.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Can my spouse work in Canada while I work or study there?</h3>
          <p>Only in certain cases since January 21, 2025. Spouses of workers qualify for an open work permit if the worker is in a TEER 0 or 1 job, or a select TEER 2 or 3 job, with at least 16 months left on their permit. Spouses of students qualify only if the student is in a master's program of 16 months or longer, a doctoral program or select professional programs. Once the principal applicant has applied for permanent residence and holds a work permit issued because of that application, the spouse may qualify under a separate measure.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">How can my family immigrate to Canada if we have a relative there?</h3>
          <p>A Canadian citizen or permanent resident can sponsor a spouse, common-law or conjugal partner, or dependent child. Parent and grandparent sponsorship is paused: IRCC stopped accepting new interest-to-sponsor forms on July 15, 2026. Brothers, sisters, aunts and uncles generally cannot sponsor you, but a brother or sister in Canada who is a citizen or permanent resident adds 15 points to an Express Entry score.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">How long does it take for a family to immigrate to Canada?</h3>
          <p>Plan for 12 to 24 months from your first language test to landing if you already qualify for a program. IRCC aims to process 80% of complete Express Entry applications in 6 months, while applications outside Express Entry usually take longer. A temporary-first route through study or work in Canada often takes several years before permanent residence.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

